[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Generics, Variance, and Constraints in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics are a foundational pillar of modern .NET, allowing developers to write highly reusable, type-safe, and performant code. Understanding variance (covariance and contravariance) and type constraints elevates your ability to design robust object-oriented and functional systems."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Introduction to Generics"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics introduce type parameters to classes, interfaces, and methods, deferring the specification of data types until the component is instantiated or invoked. This eliminates the performance overhead of boxing and unboxing associated with legacy object-based collections."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class Repository<T>
{
    private readonly List<T> _items = new List<T>();
    public void Add(T item) => _items.Add(item);
    public T GetById(int index) => _items[index];
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Type Constraints in Generics"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Constraints inform the Common Language Runtime (CLR) about the capabilities a type argument must possess. By applying constraints, you can invoke methods or safely instantiate objects of the generic type parameter."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"where T : struct - Type must be a value type."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"where T : class - Type must be a reference type."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"where T : new() - Type must have a public parameterless constructor."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"where T : BaseClass - Type must derive from a specified base class."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"where T : IInterface - Type must implement a specified interface."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public class EntityService<T> where T : class, new()
{
    public T CreateInstance()
    {
        return new T();
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Understanding Variance (Covariance and Contravariance)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Variance refers to how subtyping inheritance of generic arguments relates to the inheritance of the generic types themselves. In .NET, variance applies exclusively to generic interfaces and delegates."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Covariance (out keyword): Allows you to use a more derived type than originally specified. Covariant interfaces are strictly readonly (output-only)."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Contravariance (in keyword): Allows you to use a less derived type than originally specified. Contravariant interfaces are strictly writeonly (input-only)."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Covariance in Practice"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The out keyword marks a generic parameter as covariant. IEnumerable<T> is a classic example of a covariant interface, allowing you to assign an IEnumerable<String> to an IEnumerable<Object>."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"public interface IReadOnlyRepository<out T>
{
    T GetById(int id);
}

// Usage example:
IReadOnlyRepository<string> stringRepo = /* initialization */; 
IReadOnlyRepository<object> objectRepo = stringRepo; // Valid due to covariance (out)"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Contravariance in Practice"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"The in keyword marks a generic parameter as contravariant. This is commonly applied to delegates and comparison interfaces, such as IAction<T> or IComparer<T>, where types are consumed rather than produced."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"public interface IConsumer<in T>
{
    void Consume(T value);
}

// Usage example:
IConsumer<object> objectConsumer = /* initialization */;
IConsumer<string> stringConsumer = objectConsumer; // Valid due to contravariance (in)"},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering generics, applying precise type constraints, and leveraging variance keywords (out and in), you can design flexible, highly expressive APIs that adhere to the Liskov Substitution Principle while maintaining strict compile-time type safety."}]
